 that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.6 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.4 & 0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & 0.1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.6 & 0.9 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.8 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.6 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0.4 & -0.8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.2 & -0.9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=10{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=10{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=10{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}↔{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12 & -18 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}+6(\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}+{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}{x}_{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{6}{11} \\\\ \\frac{3}{11} \\\\ \\frac{2}{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=\\frac{6}{11}+\\frac{3}{11}-\\frac{2}{11}=\\frac{7}{11}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269075 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269075 #edb/ref 1069 "q-135530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269075 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269075 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62cbd463-845c-5e66-8284-1a8dd6e26538" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269075 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )^{2}-4(-9)) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 40}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-144}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 12\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm 6\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269220 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269220 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269220 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269220 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-5I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,(A-5I)^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269220 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "928a6016-d7ba-559e-8b6b-42abd1307642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269220 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-5I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ satisfies $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269254 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269254 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269254 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269254 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $3$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 7 & {7}^{2} & {7}^{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\beta =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269254 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bad30ca3-c18a-548d-ae2b-a1c2db90b24d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269254 #edb/ref 1074 "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\nX\\beta =y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269459 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269459 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269459 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "16bb7c90-dd67-560d-bc55-0e6d60ffa1a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269595 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -39 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269595 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269595 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24f93a4c-04c3-5326-b385-3b237af5be9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269907 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 5 \\\\ 39 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269907 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269907 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "45ce526b-cdce-5887-87fa-46e772b51158" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269962 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 39 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269962 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269962 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4d2b8368-af12-5270-8abb-2966b96811f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270004 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-39 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186270004 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270004 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52a4f459-56be-5d2b-aa62-980c1e97fec0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271532 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271532 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271532 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271532 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186269962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271532 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186270004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271532 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271532 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271532 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186269595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271532 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0f4347d9-b9dd-5363-a53a-02a14e4ccbbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272162 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450753 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272162 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450754 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272162 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450755 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272162 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272162 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272162 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A+8I$ is singular\n- **II.** $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(A+8I)=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272162 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78a8d2b4-93c9-5ecc-ada3-6436c09c3461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272162 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+8I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that if $M=A+8I$ is singular, then $(A+8I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+8I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+8I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291078 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186291078 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291078 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "68ad4ed0-c537-5d8f-aead-f1784d1197e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291219 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186291219 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291219 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8ae06e79-3bd7-5fdf-b3cd-92fb3be942fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291458 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186291458 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291458 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bfe9c811-0437-544e-8d60-7821fc519cc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291510 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186291510 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291510 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cf13d4d0-71c1-5909-8c73-82fde542f1f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291562 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186291562 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291562 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ddd5ad0a-5aa6-52bb-92c9-15cb1bf537eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291735 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186291078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291735 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186291219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291735 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186291458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291735 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186291510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291735 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186291562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291735 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291735 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291735 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186291510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186291735 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "018bfc53-7c6a-52c8-857a-7ccecc5ecb47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292561 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292561 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292561 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $M=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find $\\operatorname{ln}⁡M.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186292561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d405cd8-28c4-52fd-a2da-376dd14ddae8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186292561 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the given matrix $M,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡M=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(PJ{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $1$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡J=\\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡J=\\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) & \\frac{1}{(1)} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡M & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 4 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186300155 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300155 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300155 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "283c298b-9742-57ea-bd3e-91b19039b7fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300230 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300230 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417]